[block
  'ORD-0017-part-if'
  [ctx
    'the spec of EMS-002'
    '.guidelines/.orders/EMS-002/EMS-002.pgml'
  [/ctx]
  [tgt
    'the two proposals of the XML draft, answered on 2026-09-27: the number of a PT becomes the attribute n of part and the condition of an IF becomes the attribute cond of if, instead of each arriving as user-input; through PACKAGE_TARGET.md and conformance, with fromXML reading both in the same step; an ORD of the batch the version moves once for'
  [/tgt]
  [ctx
    'the proposals are the two the draft of release 1.4.4 got right and left for a decision, CHANGELOG.md; XML_REFERENCE.md says an attribute never carries instruction content'
  [/ctx]
  [ctx
    'measured at 80dfc4d: of the 115 declared sources three write IF and none with a literal, and no conformance vector writes PT or IF, so no hash of the snapshot and no golden moves'
  [/ctx]
  [cnst
    'the operand is the first literal that is neither raw nor a result, the one a binding takes; it moves to the attribute only when it carries nothing else, no template slot, no reference and some text, so nothing it carried is lost; otherwise it stays user-input as today'
  [/cnst]
  [cnst
    'a frame counts the literal where it was: an if with a condition does not ask for the condition'
  [/cnst]
  [cnst
    'which command takes which attribute is a table of the vocabulary, OPERAND_ATTR, read by the Rust at build time'
  [/cnst]
  [cmp
    'the attributes'
    'PT with its number emits part n and IF with its condition emits if cond, the literal gone from the children; a literal that carries a slot or a reference, or is empty, stays user-input; fromXML reads n and cond back as the literal; XML_REFERENCE and PACKAGE_TARGET state the two attributes; each seen failing before it counts, and the Rust answers byte for byte'
    [ctx[/ctx]
    [cnst[/cnst]
  [/cmp]
  [sub
    [eq
      [cmp
        [ctx[/ctx]
      [/cmp]
    [/eq]
  [/sub]
